WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.829 --> 00:00:03.511
The bar turns, and the cutting edge of a small,

2
00:00:03.651 --> 00:00:06.159
hard plate peels a chip of steel off it.

3
00:00:06.953 --> 00:00:08.938
That plate is called an insert.

4
00:00:10.035 --> 00:00:15.062
It sits in a tool holder: a steel body with a clamp, which fits into the turret.

5
00:00:16.135 --> 00:00:19.537
The edge gets hot, so the tool must stay hard when it is hot.

6
00:00:20.314 --> 00:00:20.616
Worn?

7
00:00:21.379 --> 00:00:23.922
Then you turn the insert to the next edge.

8
00:00:25.239 --> 00:00:31.264
HSS is high-speed steel: tough, cheap and easy to grind, for small drills and taps.

9
00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:36.737
Cemented carbide is made of grains of tungsten carbide, bound with cobalt.

10
00:00:37.505 --> 00:00:42.265
It stays hard when it gets hot, and cuts about three to four times faster.

11
00:00:42.959 --> 00:00:43.992
But it is brittle.

12
00:00:45.045 --> 00:00:47.530
Almost every carbide tool has a coating:

13
00:00:47.704 --> 00:00:50.212
a layer a few thousandths of a millimetre thick,

14
00:00:50.397 --> 00:00:51.744
against heat and wear.

15
00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:58.611
The box carries an ISO insert code: CNMG 12 04 08.

16
00:00:59.655 --> 00:01:02.883
C is the shape: a diamond with 80 degree corners.

17
00:01:03.598 --> 00:01:07.162
N: no clearance angle, so both faces can be used.

18
00:01:07.937 --> 00:01:12.906
M is the tolerance class, and G: a hole for clamping, and chip breakers.

19
00:01:13.929 --> 00:01:18.132
12 is the cutting edge length in whole millimetres: for this diamond,

20
00:01:18.352 --> 00:01:21.649
an inscribed circle of 12.7 millimetres.

21
00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.090
04 is the thickness: 4.76 millimetres.

22
00:01:26.802 --> 00:01:31.191
And 08 is the nose radius: 0.8 millimetres.

23
00:01:32.191 --> 00:01:35.558
Two corners per face, two faces: 4 edges.

24
00:01:36.272 --> 00:01:42.332
At €14 an insert, that is 3 euros 50 an edge, and at 70 parts an edge,

25
00:01:42.506 --> 00:01:43.760
5 cents a part.

26
00:01:45.136 --> 00:01:48.596
The box also names the grade: the carbide and the coating,

27
00:01:48.735 --> 00:01:50.569
chosen for a material group.

28
00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:57.499
P blue is steel, M yellow stainless steel, K red cast iron, N green aluminium,

29
00:01:57.697 --> 00:02:02.422
S brown titanium and heat-resistant alloys, and H grey hardened steel.

30
00:02:03.428 --> 00:02:06.643
A P grade in stainless steel works, but badly.

31
00:02:07.319 --> 00:02:09.420
Does the grade on the box match this job?

32
00:02:10.743 --> 00:02:15.573
With flank wear, a dull band grows along the cutting edge: slowly at first,

33
00:02:15.921 --> 00:02:17.535
then faster and faster.

34
00:02:18.237 --> 00:02:23.125
With crater wear, a hollow wears into the top, where the hot chip slides over it.

35
00:02:24.201 --> 00:02:29.368
On aluminium and soft steel, material welds onto the edge: a built-up edge.

36
00:02:30.067 --> 00:02:32.494
That spoils the size and the surface.

37
00:02:33.511 --> 00:02:36.123
You see wear in drifting sizes and burrs.

38
00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:40.309
And the spindle load rises: the motor has to work harder.

39
00:02:41.618 --> 00:02:46.471
Emulsion cools, lubricates and flushes the chips away: oil in water,

40
00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:48.781
usually 5 to 8 percent oil.

41
00:02:49.764 --> 00:02:54.025
With presetting you measure the length and radius of a tool outside the machine,

42
00:02:54.176 --> 00:02:55.848
so the machine keeps cutting.

43
00:02:56.876 --> 00:03:01.577
When the tool life is up, a sister tool takes over: an identical spare tool.

44
00:03:02.345 --> 00:03:09.903
A €12 insert with 4 edges is €3 an edge: at 80 parts, 3.75 cents a part.

45
00:03:11.276 --> 00:03:14.109
The Taylor equation links tool life to speed:

46
00:03:14.248 --> 00:03:17.685
V times T to the power n equals a constant C.

47
00:03:18.655 --> 00:03:23.020
The Taylor exponent n is often about 0.25 for carbide.

48
00:03:23.853 --> 00:03:31.155
Then 25 percent faster, from 200 to 250, leaves only 0.41 of the tool life.

49
00:03:32.166 --> 00:03:36.856
At 200 metres a minute an edge lasts 15 minutes: 30 parts.

50
00:03:37.574 --> 00:03:42.961
At 220 that becomes 10.2 minutes, and about 22 parts.

51
00:03:43.981 --> 00:03:45.305
In a tool-life test,

52
00:03:45.467 --> 00:03:50.889
the wear criterion is usually a wear land of 0.3 millimetres on average.

53
00:03:51.893 --> 00:03:54.459
And tool monitoring watches the spindle load,

54
00:03:54.598 --> 00:03:56.978
and stops the machine when a tool breaks.

55
00:03:58.318 --> 00:04:01.883
A part costs machine time, tool changes and tools.

56
00:04:02.601 --> 00:04:06.142
Cutting faster shrinks the machine time, but grows the rest.

57
00:04:07.168 --> 00:04:09.142
The change time is 2 minutes.

58
00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:15.584
An edge of €4, at 1 euro 50 a minute, is 2.67 machine minutes.

59
00:04:16.308 --> 00:04:18.595
Together, 4.67 minutes.

60
00:04:19.631 --> 00:04:25.702
The economic tool life is 1 over n, minus 1, times those 4.67 minutes.

61
00:04:26.428 --> 00:04:32.187
With n at 0.25, that is 3 times 4.67: 14 minutes.

62
00:04:33.183 --> 00:04:38.930
Taylor then gives the economic cutting speed: C divided by the fourth root of 14,

63
00:04:39.081 --> 00:04:42.982
about 204 metres a minute: where the cost is lowest.

64
00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:49.312
With a sister tool, the stop drops to 0.3 minutes: 8.9 minutes,

65
00:04:49.485 --> 00:04:52.120
at 228 metres a minute.

66
00:04:52.856 --> 00:04:55.619
Faster only pays when changing is cheap.

67
00:04:56.948 --> 00:05:00.768
The bigger money is often in tool standardisation: fewer kinds,

68
00:05:00.942 --> 00:05:04.692
the same on every machine, and cutting data everyone knows.

69
00:05:05.689 --> 00:05:07.918
So don’t chase the cheapest insert.

70
00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:10.113
Chase the cheapest part.
